      {
        "id": "p17-q7-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Show that any multiple of 12 is abundant.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Every positive multiple $12k$ is abundant.\n\nIts proper divisors include $k,2k,3k,4k,6k$, whose sum is $16k>12k$.",
          "worked": "Let the positive multiple be $12k$, where $k\\in\\mathbb N$. The distinct numbers $k,2k,3k,4k,6k$ are all proper divisors of $12k$. If $D$ is the sum of all its proper divisors, then\n$$\\begin{align*}D&\\ge k+2k+3k+4k+6k\\\\D&\\ge16k\\\\D&>12k.\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore, $12k$ is abundant.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 32
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q7-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Let $p$ be a prime number and $n\\in\\mathbb N$. Prove that $p^n$ is not abundant.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$p^n$ is not abundant.\n\nIts proper-divisor sum is $D=\\dfrac{p^n-1}{p-1}$, and $p^n-D=\\dfrac{(p-2)p^n+1}{p-1}>0$.",
          "worked": "Since $p$ is prime, the proper divisors of $p^n$ are $1,p,p^2,\\ldots,p^{n-1}$. Their sum is\n$$\\begin{align*}D&=1+p+p^2+\\cdots+p^{n-1}\\\\D&=\\frac{p^n-1}{p-1}.\\end{align*}$$\nCompare this sum with $p^n$:\n$$\\begin{align*}p^n-D&=p^n-\\frac{p^n-1}{p-1}\\\\p^n-D&=\\frac{p^n(p-1)-(p^n-1)}{p-1}\\\\p^n-D&=\\frac{(p-2)p^n+1}{p-1}\\\\p^n-D&>0.\\end{align*}$$\nThe last inequality holds because $p\\ge2$. Thus $D<p^n$, so $p^n$ is not abundant.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 48
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q7-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Let $p>2$ be a prime number and $n\\in\\mathbb N$. Prove that if $p<2^{n+1}-1$, then $2^np$ is abundant.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$2^np$ is abundant.\n\nIts proper-divisor sum is $D=(2^{n+1}-1)+p(2^n-1)>p+p(2^n-1)=2^np$.",
          "worked": "Since $p$ is an odd prime, the divisors of $2^np$ are $1,2,4,\\ldots,2^n$ and $p,2p,4p,\\ldots,2^np$. Only $2^np$ is not a proper divisor. Hence their proper-divisor sum is\n$$\\begin{align*}D&=(1+2+\\cdots+2^n)+p(1+2+\\cdots+2^{n-1})\\\\D&=(2^{n+1}-1)+p(2^n-1).\\end{align*}$$\nUsing $2^{n+1}-1>p$,\n$$\\begin{align*}D&>p+p(2^n-1)\\\\D&>p+2^np-p\\\\D&>2^np.\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore, $2^np$ is abundant.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 48
      }
    ]
